  <h1>Signs Your Termination May Have Been Wrongful</h1>

  <p>
    Most people who are fired assume — or are told — that the termination was legitimate. Employers rarely say "we fired you because of your age" or "this is retaliation for your EEOC complaint." Instead, they offer neutral-sounding explanations: restructuring, performance issues, budget cuts, cultural fit. But these stated reasons are not always the real reasons — and employment law allows employees to challenge them.
  </p>
  <p>
    The following are red flags that your termination may have been illegal. None of these alone proves a wrongful termination claim, but each one is the kind of evidence an employment attorney will evaluate carefully when assessing whether to take your case.
  </p>

  <h2>1. You Were Fired Shortly After a Protected Activity</h2>
  <p>
    Timing is one of the most powerful pieces of circumstantial evidence in a retaliation case. If you were fired within weeks or a few months of engaging in a protected activity — filing an EEOC complaint, reporting harassment or discrimination to HR, requesting FMLA leave, filing a workers' compensation claim, making an OSHA safety complaint, or blowing the whistle on illegal conduct — the timing alone can create an inference of retaliation.
  </p>
  <p>
    Courts have found that terminations occurring within days to a few weeks of protected activity can support a retaliation inference. Longer gaps (6+ months) are harder to connect to retaliation unless there is other evidence of the employer's motive.
  </p>

  <h2>2. The Employer's Explanation Doesn't Add Up</h2>
  <p>
    Employers routinely change their story about why someone was fired. If the reason you were given at termination is different from what your employer later tells the EEOC or states in litigation, that inconsistency is evidence of pretext — a cover story designed to hide the real, illegal reason. Watch for:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>The stated reason at termination differs from what is in your termination letter</li>
    <li>HR has told different things to different people</li>
    <li>The employer adds new reasons over time that were never mentioned at the time of firing</li>
    <li>The performance issues cited were never documented before the termination decision</li>
    <li>You received positive performance reviews and then were suddenly fired for performance</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>3. You Were Treated Differently From Coworkers Outside Your Protected Class</h2>
  <p>
    Comparator evidence — showing that similarly situated employees outside the protected class were treated better — is one of the most powerful forms of discrimination evidence. If you were fired for conduct (tardiness, a policy violation, a mistake) that other employees who are a different race, sex, age, or religion engaged in without consequence, that disparity can support an inference of discriminatory motive.
  </p>
  <p>
    "Similarly situated" requires that the comparators had the same supervisor, were subject to the same workplace standards, had similar job functions, and engaged in similar or worse conduct without being fired. The comparison doesn't need to be perfect — but the more similar the circumstances, the stronger the evidence.
  </p>

  <h2>4. You Were Fired Shortly After Requesting or Taking FMLA Leave</h2>
  <p>
    FMLA interference and retaliation are among the most clearly recognized wrongful termination theories. If you requested or were on FMLA leave for a serious health condition, a family member's serious health condition, or the birth or adoption of a child, and your employment was terminated during or shortly after that leave, you may have an FMLA claim.
  </p>
  <p>
    This is particularly true if the employer: denied your FMLA request without a legitimate basis; failed to give you required FMLA notices; counted your FMLA absences as attendance violations; or used your leave as the triggering event for a performance improvement plan or termination.
  </p>

  <h2>5. Your Pregnancy, Disability, or Medical Condition Was Mentioned Around the Time of Firing</h2>
  <p>
    If your employer made comments about your pregnancy, physical limitations, medical condition, age, or other protected characteristic — even offhand or in jest — in proximity to your termination, those comments can be significant evidence. Courts call these "stray remarks," but they are not always legally irrelevant. Statements by decision-makers about protected characteristics, made close in time to the termination, carry particularly heavy evidentiary weight.
  </p>

  <h2>6. You Were the Only Member of a Protected Group Let Go in a "Restructuring"</h2>
  <p>
    Reductions in force (RIFs) and restructurings are often legitimate — but they can also be used to disguise discriminatory terminations. Red flags include: only employees of a particular protected class (older workers, women, workers of a particular race) being selected for layoff; the stated selection criteria being vague or subjective; employees being terminated and replaced by someone outside the protected class; or the selection process being handled by someone with a documented bias.
  </p>

  <h2>7. Your Employer Violated Its Own Policies</h2>
  <p>
    Most employers have written policies governing progressive discipline, performance improvement plans, termination procedures, and investigations. If the employer bypassed these procedures in your case — firing you without following the progressive discipline steps, without the documented warnings called for in the handbook, or without the investigation the policy requires — that deviation is evidence that the stated reason for your firing may not be the real one.
  </p>
  <p>
    As someone with HR experience, I can tell you that the strongest wrongful termination cases are often not those with smoking-gun discrimination evidence — they're the ones where the employer's own documented procedures were quietly ignored when it came time to fire someone in a protected class.
  </p>

  <h2>8. You Were Asked to Sign a Broad Release Quickly After the Firing</h2>
  <p>
    Employers who know or suspect a firing could produce legal liability often pressure employees to sign broad severance agreements immediately after termination. If you were offered severance in exchange for a release of claims, and particularly if you were pressured to sign quickly, that urgency itself suggests your employer was trying to extinguish potential liability.
  </p>
  <p>
    Do not sign any separation agreement or release of claims without having an employment attorney review it first.
  </p>

